The cache-aside pattern in Java with Spring and Redis:

```java
@Service
public class ProductService {

    private final ProductRepository repo;
    private final RedisTemplate<String, Product> redis;

    // Cache-aside READ: check cache, fallback to DB, populate cache
    public Product findById(Long id) {
        String key = "product:" + id;
        Product cached = redis.opsForValue().get(key);
        if (cached != null) {
            return cached; // Cache hit — skip the DB entirely
        }
        // Cache miss — load from database
        Product product = repo.findById(id)
            .orElseThrow(() -> new ProductNotFoundException(id));
        // Populate cache with TTL so stale entries auto-expire
        redis.opsForValue().set(key, product, Duration.ofMinutes(15));
        return product;
    }

    // Cache-aside WRITE: update DB first, then evict stale cache
    public Product update(Product product) {
        Product saved = repo.save(product);
        redis.delete("product:" + saved.getId()); // Evict, don't update
        return saved;
    }
}
```

**Why evict instead of update on write?** Eviction is simpler and avoids race conditions where a concurrent read between the DB write and cache update could re-populate the cache with stale data. The next read will miss the cache and fetch the fresh value from the DB.

With Spring annotations, the same pattern becomes declarative:

```java
@Cacheable("products")   // Cache-aside read
public Product findById(Long id) { return repo.findById(id).orElseThrow(); }

@CacheEvict("products")  // Evict on write
public void update(Product p) { repo.save(p); }
```
